{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And you shall say to him, HaShem, etc., has sent me, etc.</b> The entire verse seems superfluous, for He need only have stated the verse after it, ״כֹּה אָמַר ה׳״ etc. And if He meant to say that He sent him, this is unnecessary, for it is already known. Perhaps, however, since he came to him while he was going to attend to his needs early in the morning, Pharaoh might say that this is not proper etiquette, that he should have waited until the time when he would be sitting in his palace and then deliver the message to him — therefore HaShem commanded him to give him a reason at the start of his words, saying to him: HaShem <b>has sent me to you</b>, and the word of the King is upon me to come at this time. Moreover, since He is the supreme El, His mission must be carried out immediately." "<b>Further</b>, it intends according to the statement of our Sages, of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 8:1), on the verse (Ezekiel 29:3): ״לִי יְאוֹרִי וַאֲנִי עֲשִׂיתִנִי״ — that he made himself out to be a god, and he would deceive his people, saying that he brings in the water but does not let it out, and he would go out early in the morning, hidden from all living beings, and attend to his needs in the Nile. Therefore the mission from HaShem came to the place of his stealth and hiding from all living beings, and he said to him at the start of his words: ״HaShem, <b>Elohim of the Hebrews</b>, <b>has sent me to you</b>״ — meaning, He made known to me the place where you are, and here is a sign for you that HaShem our Elohim knows if a man hides himself in hiding places. Moreover, through this he was put to shame, for HaShem revealed his disgrace to His servants, for just as he swallows it so he excretes it, and falsehood is at his right hand, and behold he is as one sweeps away dung (I Kings 14:10). And His saying <b>and behold, you have not hearkened until now</b> — meaning, He informs him that HaShem knows thoughts, and even though Pharaoh had not yet given Moses an answer concerning the matter, as I explained in the verse ״כָּבֵד לֵב פַּרְעֹה״, even so, HaShem knows that he has not hearkened until now." "<b>Further</b>, it intends that since the mission of Moses had preceded and he performed the sign of the staff that turned into a serpent, and there was no plague when he did not hearken through that mission, when he came to strike the first plague of blood, he said to him that the mission had already told him what evil he was bringing upon him. And this is the reading of the verse: <b>HaShem, Elohim of the Hebrews</b> — <b>has already sent me to you</b> and I have spoken to you, and behold, you have not hearkened; therefore <b>thus says HaShem</b> etc. — in such a manner that there is no need to say to him yet another time, ״כֹּה אָמַר ה׳ שַׁלַּח עַמִּי״ etc., for he had already told him the matter."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:25:44.915023655Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}